topics.simpleExplanation:
Because functions are first-class citizens, we can pass function `y` into function `x`:

```js
function x(y) {
  console.log('x');
  y();
}
x(function y() {
  console.log('y');
});
```

**Event Listeners & Garbage Collection**:
Event listeners form closures over their outer variables. If a page has 1,000 DOM buttons with attached event listeners closing over large scope data, that memory **cannot be garbage collected** while the DOM elements exist.

Removing event listeners (`removeEventListener`) frees up memory when elements are unmounted.

codeExamples.code:
function attachEventListener() {
  let count = 0;
  const button = document.getElementById('clickMe');

  function handleClick() {
    console.log('Button Clicked', ++count);
  }

  button.addEventListener('click', handleClick);

  // Return cleanup function for memory management
  return function remove() {
    button.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);
  };
}

