topics.simpleExplanation:
Two major issues with asynchronous callbacks:

1. **Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom)**:
   Asynchronous operations growing horizontally instead of vertically:
   ```js
   createOrder(cart, function(orderId) {
     proceedToPayment(orderId, function(paymentInfo) {
       showOrderSummary(paymentInfo, function() {
         updateWallet();
       });
     });
   });
   ```
2. **Inversion of Control (IoC)**:
   When you pass a callback function to `createOrder()`, you yield control to `createOrder`'s implementation. If `createOrder` has a bug, it might call your callback **0 times, twice, or with invalid arguments** (e.g. charging credit card twice!).

codeExamples.code:
// Anti-Pattern: Inversion of Control & Pyramid of Doom
api.createOrder(cart, function (orderId) {
  api.proceedToPayment(orderId, function (paymentStatus) {
    api.showSummary(paymentStatus, function () {
      api.updateBalance();
    });
  });
});

